WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.502
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.270
you know from films like
"Reminiscence" and "Dumbo."

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.705
You can see her
in "The Last of Us,"

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.874
which premieres January 15th
on HBO.

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:09.208
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.877
-They're saying it's a virus,
some kind of parasite.

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:15.948
-Is it from terrorists?
-We don't know.

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:18.284
-Are we sick?
-No, of course not.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:21.888
-Why did things blow up?
-No cellphone, no radio.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:23.389
Minute ago,
the newsman wouldn't shut up.

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:25.258
-How do you know?
-What?

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:27.727
-How do you know we're not sick?

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:30.496
-Please welcome to the show
Nico Parker, everyone.

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.331
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:42.341 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:45.045 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:47.380
Hi! How are you?
-I'm good. How are you?

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.148
-I'm wonderful.
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:50.817
-Thank you for having me.
-This is a film

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.419
based on a very popular
video game.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.054
-Yes.
-Post-apocalyptic.

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:57.557
You got to see it at a premiere
with an audience full of people.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.559
Was it really fun?
-We did.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:01.361
We watched the first episode,

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:03.296
which, you know,
I'm prevalent in.

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:06.966
And it was amazing,
but I suck at watching myself.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.502
I hate nothing more.
So I'm --

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.137
You know, I'm sitting there,
and I'm, like, gearing up.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:13.072
You know, some people leave
as soon as it starts

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.207
because they just can't face it.
But I was thinking like,

00:01:15.207 --> 00:01:17.944
"If I'm in the room,
no one can be like,

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:20.980
'God, she was so bad,'
because I'm right next to them,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.815
so I was like,
"I should just be there

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.884
just so that no one
can say anything."

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.086
-That's very smart.
Did you stand up

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:29.188
before it started and say,
"Just so you all know,

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:31.924
I'm gonna be staying"?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:33.960
-I was wearing a very bright
dress purposefully

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.027
so I could be like,
"Do you all see

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.396
that I'm right here
in front of you?"

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:40.300
-This is based on a pandemic,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.836
obviously a bit more severe than
the one we just went through.

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.337
How did you spend your pandemic?

00:01:45.337 --> 00:01:46.972
-Um, it was super relaxed.

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:49.975
I was at home in London
with my family.

00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:52.411
I was doing the typical
school Zooming,

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.814
which was amazing
'cause you join the maths Zoom,

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:57.083
and then you mute it and you go
downstairs for a couple of hours

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.518
and then you come back,
and it's finished.

00:01:59.518 --> 00:02:01.921
-Wow!
What a celebration of education.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.490
-It was incredible.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.125
But I'd come back,
and they'd be like,

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:07.927
"Why are you still here
three hours later?"

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.363
Like, "Sorry. I just really
lost track of the equations."

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.198
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.100
But it was a good
learning lesson for me

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.436
'cause it taught me that me and
my sister cannot live together.

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:20.606
Butting heads was intense.
But it was great.

00:02:20.606 --> 00:02:22.141
-Is your sister older
or younger?

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.911
-Older.
-Okay. I heard a story

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.979
that maybe one of the reasons
she's tricky to live with is

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:30.549
because she's brutally honest,
because I heard that she met,

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:32.084
at the time,
Prime Minister Boris Johnson

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:33.085
in the U.K.
-Yes.

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:34.620
-And what did she say to him?

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.690
-She was at "Harry Potter
and The Cursed Child,"

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:38.757
which is her safe space.

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:40.360
And she saw him,
and she went up,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.261
and she tapped him
on the shoulder, and she went,

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:44.397
"Um, excuse me, Mr. Johnson,"
and he turned around,

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.864
was like, "Yes?"

00:02:45.864 --> 00:02:47.833
I think he was expecting
a compliment of some sort.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:50.670
And she goes, "You're a [bleep]
Enjoy the show."

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:58.544
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:01.314
-It's very nice.
It's very sweet that she also

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.783
wanted him to enjoy the show.
-That is her way, yeah.

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:07.220
Yeah.
She's an absolute legend, yeah.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.856
-You -- Your parents
are both in the business.

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:13.959
Thandiwe Newton, a fantastic
actor in her own right.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:17.663
And then your father
is a film director, Ol Parker.

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:20.533
And tell me, are they --

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.803
When you audition for things,
do you get feedback from them?

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:26.772
What has life been like
with them in the same career?

00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:30.510
-It's -- It's amazing, I think,
because me and my dad

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.345
do a lot of self-tapes together
which is great,

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.414
'cause it's just like cheating.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:37.750
And so we do
a lot of self-tapes together.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.485
So I've gotten way more
comfortable.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:41.020
-So this is when you have
to audition for a role

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.388
and you can't be there
in person,

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.490
you get an actual film director
to film yours, right.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.826
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.826 --> 00:03:50.463
-But so, you know, me and him,
he's very good at kind of

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:52.598
keeping it parent
and then professional,

00:03:52.598 --> 00:03:54.934
very good at that dynamic.
But even after, you know,

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.235
been doing it
a couple years now,

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.938
and after every single
first take, I kind of sit there,

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.072
and we both kind of
sit in silence,

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:01.473
and I'm like, "How was it?
You hated it!

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:02.542
I know you hated it!"

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:05.044
And we just have that
kind of struggle.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.912
But it's great.
They're both very good

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.981
at kind of being
the supportive parents

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:10.816
and then also giving, like,
professional criticism

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:13.285
and praise and all of that.
-Have you been lucky enough,

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:16.088
or did you feel,
as a young person,

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:17.856
was it lucky to go to sets
that they were working on?

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:19.358
Did you like those visits?

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:22.628
-You know, I have to say they
were boring when I was younger.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:24.864
It's a lot of waiting.
-Yeah.

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.632
They're boring when
you get older, too, yeah.

00:04:26.632 --> 00:04:27.866
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:30.136
-But I was like, "You guys
just don't do any work."

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:33.305
Just a lot of waiting.
But it was, you know,

00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:36.108
the difference in sets with both
my parents is extreme

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:37.610
'cause, you know, with my dad,
I'm going onto

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.679
the "Mamma Mia!" set, and I'm
like, "Hey, Cher, hey Meryl."

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:42.615
Just like, you know,
with all the A-listers.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:45.684
And then, with my mom,
it was the "Westworld" set,

00:04:45.684 --> 00:04:48.721
and you are just surrounded
by all these corpses.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.123
-Yeah.
-Me and my sister --

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:52.759
They're all prosthetic,
obviously.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:55.628
And we -- my dad,
we were just bored and like,

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:56.796
"We want to go home."
My dad's like,

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:58.163
"Go play over there
with that dead body."

00:04:58.163 --> 00:05:00.599
[ Laughter ]
So we see this kind of

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:03.903
corpse on a stretcher
with a sheet over it.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.437
Me and my sister go over,
and we peel back.

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:08.607
And it's my mom, a prosthetic
version of my mother

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:11.511
looking incredibly --
-There's the lovely Thandiwe.

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:13.946
I mean, this is really
a good example

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:15.815
of how little she's working.
I mean...

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.783
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:19.752
That is not a fun thing to see.

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:21.520
Hey, congratulations so much
on the show.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:22.721
-Thank you very much.
-And thank you so much

00:05:22.721 --> 00:05:24.090
for being here.
It's been lovely talking to you.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.558
-Thank you for having me.
-Nico Parker, everybody.

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:29.261
"The Last of Us" premieres
January 15th on HBO.

00:05:29.261 --> 00:05:31.898
We'll be right back with music
from Inhaler.

